{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a296f0fd554276dec1a064589d0a20afb6df4db26f87cc3fb2aff40b766132f4"} {"question" "q-49661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "The sequence diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n>1,$ diverge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a296f0fd554276dec1a064589d0a20afb6df4db26f87cc3fb2aff40b766132f4"} {"question" "q-49734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only"] ["text" "${a}_{n}$ only"] ["text" "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only"] ["text" "${b}_{n}$ only"] ["text" "${c}_{n}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-49734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${a}_{n}$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-75948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10$ for $n\\ge 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-75948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=10+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-75948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a296f0fd554276dec1a064589d0a20afb6df4db26f87cc3fb2aff40b766132f4"} {"question" "q-75948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-75948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["text" "The sequence diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-75948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The sequence diverges"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a296f0fd554276dec1a064589d0a20afb6df4db26f87cc3fb2aff40b766132f4"} {"question" "q-123707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "100"] ["math" "3"] ["text" "The sequence diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1$ for $n\\ge 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{n/10}}{{log}_{5}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a296f0fd554276dec1a064589d0a20afb6df4db26f87cc3fb2aff40b766132f4"} {"question" "q-123714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["text" "The sequence diverges."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"} {"question" "q-123714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The sequence diverges."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979971/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1bb8375671a8b062cc9af6b3b922c0087727b6155e6be31469ebd58e25810bc"}] "usage" {"q-49661" []} "basis" 864 "retractions" [[17592186101363 :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_{n} = \\frac{\\ln n}{n^{2}}$ for $n \\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186099775 17592186099776 17592186099777 17592186099778 17592186099779] "immutable_fields" [[17592186099774 :answer-field/correct] [17592186099774 :answer-field/id] [17592186099774 :answer-field/key] [17592186099774 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
